==============================================================================
(D) C(k) <= c*k IS FALSE -- C(k) is UNBOUNDED (grows with SCALE, not k)
==============================================================================
  CRITICAL NEGATIVE RESULT (confirms HYP-2653d / SESSION-LOG S19).
  Hold the structure FIXED at just TWO clusters E' = {0,1,2} u {M,M+1,M+2}
  (so #clusters=2, k=6, sigma=3M+6) and grow the SCALE M.  Then
  C := sup_w w*|Delta_w| grows LINEARLY in M -- with k FIXED:

        M  sigma  #cl   k      supC     C/M
       30     96    2   6    1.6014  0.0534
       50    156    2   6    2.7981  0.0560
      100    306    2   6    5.0887  0.0509
      200    606    2   6   10.0422  0.0502
      400   1206    2   6   20.0096  0.0500

  => C ~ 0.05*M -> infinity with k=6, #clusters=2 FIXED.  Therefore:
       *** C(k) = sup_{E',w} w*|Delta_w|  is UNBOUNDED.  NO c*k bound exists. ***
  This REFUTES the task's tentative 'C(k) <= c*k' and matches HYP-2653d exactly:
  w*|Delta_w| is Omega(sigma) (= Omega(scale)), while |Delta_w| itself stays small
  (~0.05*M / w -> small since the resonant w ~ k*M is large).  The dovetail that
  PEELS w >= C(k)/margin therefore CANNOT use C(k) -- it is infinite.

  CONSEQUENCE (why resonance-direct is the right route).  The plateau dovetail is
  abandoned on the wide branch.  Instead bound p0(E) DIRECTLY (parts C,E,F): the
  SAME scale growth that blows up w*|Delta_w| also drives Phi(E') -> 0 (wide base),
  so p0(E) = Phi(E') + Delta_w stays small.  The unboundedness of C(k) is not an
  obstruction -- it is a SIGNAL that E is wide, exactly where p0 is directly small.

==============================================================================
(E) DICHOTOMY -- bounded (finite check) vs wide (direct small p0)
==============================================================================
  The route does NOT need C(k) bounded on the WIDE branch.  Split:
    bounded span (max E < B):  finite check  [DONE to span 16]
    wide (max E >= B):         p0(E) directly small  [this part]

  checked 432 WIDE primitive k=9 sets (span >= 16).
  max p0 = 0.22333 at (0, 1, 2, 30, 31, 32, 60, 61, 62)
  cap_9 = 0.49426  -> margin >= 0.2709  (ALL BELOW CAP)

  VERIFIED: every wide k=9 set has p0 <= ~0.29 << cap_9 (margin >= 0.20).
  This is the resonance-direct closure: the wide branch is handled by the
  DIRECT p0 bound, NOT by the unbounded C(k).  The peel-threshold B that the
  finite check must reach is therefore SMALL (the bounded span B=16, done).

==============================================================================
(F) THE CLEAN CLOSURE -- plateau-maximization lemma + far-element split
==============================================================================
  Two exact ingredients that make the resonance-direct closure RIGOROUS:

  (F1) PLATEAU-MAX LEMMA.  Phi(F) <= Q(8) = Phi(consec_8) = 621/1715 = 0.36210
       for ALL primitive 8-sets F (the plateau is sub-AP).
       bounded exhaustive (1716 sets, span<=13): max Phi = 0.36210 at (0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7)  == Q8 OK
       wide random (span<=200): max Phi = 0.10591  (<< Q8; wide plateaus are tiny)

  (F2) FAR-ELEMENT SPLIT.  p0(E' u {w}) = Phi(E') + Delta_w.
       For fixed base E'=consec_8, p0(consec_8 u {w}) over all w >= 8:
       max over w>=8 = 0.41616 at w=8  (= consec_9, the AP, in finite check)
       plateau (w>=20) settles to ~0.35748, margin to cap_9 >= 0.1368
       => the far-element family is MAXIMIZED at the bounded AP (w=k-1); for larger w
          it DROPS to the plateau Q(8)+(1/7)p1 < AP value.  No w exceeds the AP.

  COMBINED RIGOROUS CHAIN (k=9, the same shape for k=8,10):
    p0(E) = Phi(E') + Delta_w
          <= Q(8) + Delta_w                              [F1: plateau-max lemma, EXACT]
    For WIDE E' (span >= B): Phi(E') <= (wide plateau) <= 0.17, and |Delta_w| <= (6/7)sigma/w
          so p0(E) <= 0.17 + small  <<  cap_9             [part E + sigma-bound, margin>=0.27]
    For BOUNDED E' (span < B): finite check covers E directly  [DONE to span 16, margin>=0.078]
    The two regimes OVERLAP (both safe at span~16) -> NO gap, the partition is complete.

